Byron Arnold wrote:
>
> Yesterday, I finally reached the Oracle. Farthest I have ever made it.
> Of course, I bypassed the Gnomish Mines (I had read that the game is
> easier if you put off the mines until after Sokoban. Can anyone
> confirm/repusiate this for me?).

I can't confirm either way.  Some prefer one order some prefer the other
ordre.  No right or wrong answer.  Whichever you do chances are hunger
will no longer be a problem for the rest of the game once you've done a
couple of levels of the branch.

One key to surviving the mines is to cycle up and down the stairs.  Take
your pet down.  Kill a couple of monsters and go back up the stairs to
heal before you have lost half of your hit points.  Go back down the
stairs after you are completely healed.  Don't pick up anything but a
fresh corpse to eat as soon as you get back up. After the crowd thins a
lot it is time to explore the entire level and think about stashing
the items you find. First goal is to learn to survive the first level
consistantly. Second goal is to find a balance where you kill roughly
the same number of monsters as your pet so you both grow in hit
points. Third goal is to larn how to dance around a peaceful while
your pet kills it and you get the items. Forth goal is to learn how to
use your pet to figure out which items to try and which items to not
try.  By the time you get to mine town another goal is to learn how to
get your pet away from a monster too powerful for it to fight.  I've
lost a lot of pets to the watch captain.  Certain peacefuls are better
handled by your pet on the way back up not on the way down.

The other key surviving almost everywhere is Elbereth.

And missile combat.  To be learned and maximized.

More on cycling up and down stairs.  Learn to retreat more.  You can't
die in the middle of a crowd if you never encounter a group of monsters
except in a corridor.  And in a corridor you can swap with your pet to
have time to heal.

> It was no fun once I got there,
> though. While dipping my long sword in the fountains (I was a level 5
> lawful knight), I called up a nymph who stole my (very rusty) sword.

Yep.  You have to be able to handle a nymph before it's safe to dip for
Excalibur.  I tend to wait until I'm level 7 then I go back to level 1
and dip every fountain in the mines until I have Excalibur or run out of
fountains.  Just before I start dipping I explore down to the Oracle to
make sure there are enough.  It matters a lot to have a good alternate
weapon before the dipping starts.

> Then there was another nymph,

The other ones start out sleeping.  At first avoid making enough noise
to wake them up.

> and a leprechaun.

Learn to rob shops and you can stop caring about gold.  Learn to leave
the gold on the ground and only gather it to your stash just before you
go down the stairs to start exploring a level and you have less time
carrying much gold.

